WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.067
Golden datasets.

2
00:00:02.367 --> 00:00:05.867
Every automated metric needs something to calibrate against.

3
00:00:05.867 --> 00:00:07.033
This is that anchor.

4
00:00:08.233 --> 00:00:14.133
A golden dataset is a small, hand-verified, versioned set of inputs and expected outputs.

5
00:00:14.433 --> 00:00:18.567
Golden means a human checked every example against the source of truth.

6
00:00:18.867 --> 00:00:23.300
No scraped labels, and no model-generated ground truth you didn't verify.

7
00:00:24.500 --> 00:00:26.167
Three habits keep it trustworthy.

8
00:00:26.467 --> 00:00:27.432
Keep it in git.

9
00:00:27.432 --> 00:00:31.167
A changed expectation is a diff that someone must approve.

10
00:00:31.467 --> 00:00:32.499
Record provenance.

11
00:00:32.499 --> 00:00:35.767
Where did the truth come from, who verified it, and when?

12
00:00:36.067 --> 00:00:37.329
Grow it deliberately.

13
00:00:37.329 --> 00:00:40.033
Fix the bug, then keep the failure as a test.

14
00:00:41.233 --> 00:00:42.600
Why does it matter so much?

15
00:00:42.900 --> 00:00:45.000
Because the golden set sits at the centre.

16
00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:46.933
Regression gates read from it.

17
00:00:47.233 --> 00:00:49.600
Judge models are calibrated against it.

18
00:00:49.900 --> 00:00:51.692
And benchmarks trace back to it.

19
00:00:51.692 --> 00:00:55.667
If the anchor is wrong, everything downstream measures the wrong thing.

20
00:00:56.867 --> 00:00:58.567
Here is one golden example.

21
00:00:58.867 --> 00:01:02.633
It has the input and the expected output a human verified.

22
00:01:02.933 --> 00:01:07.733
It also records its provenance, where the truth came from, plus the version it was added.

23
00:01:08.033 --> 00:01:13.267
And a simple assert on the size makes any change to the set loud, so someone has to review it.

24
00:01:14.467 --> 00:01:15.533
Here's the payoff.

25
00:01:15.833 --> 00:01:23.405
You pick the twenty five most representative real user questions and answer each by hand, against primary reports.

26
00:01:23.405 --> 00:01:24.600
About three hours.

27
00:01:24.900 --> 00:01:30.067
Weeks later a teammate fixes a chunking bug, and two golden expectations start failing.

28
00:01:30.367 --> 00:01:35.610
The discussion shows the expectations were right, and the fix broke section headers.

29
00:01:35.610 --> 00:01:40.167
A failed CI run and a five minute revert, instead of a silent regression.

30
00:01:41.367 --> 00:01:42.161
Let's recap.

31
00:01:42.161 --> 00:01:46.067
A golden set is hand verified, versioned, and lives in git.

32
00:01:46.367 --> 00:01:50.900
Quality beats quantity: twenty five you trust beat twenty five hundred you don't.

33
00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:53.300
Record provenance for every example.

34
00:01:53.600 --> 00:01:56.769
And turn each production failure into a new test.

35
00:01:56.769 --> 00:01:59.033
Next up: hallucination measurement.
